  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    The Thron∴mbra Fracta was the succession crisis that fractured the Collective☽ Of Led∴merch, a Nightshelf merchant guild controlling shadow-trade and fire-core logistics from the deep tunnels. It occurred during the Frozen Peace and centered on ☽'shadow Umbrsulcavus~, the anomaly-cavern where the Berg's ice preserves memories of abnormal depth and intensity.
    
    The Collective's leader descended into Umbrsulcavus~ and did not return — dying not in battle but in the ice-silence where the oldest memories are preserved. Three claimants who had waited at the cavern threshold each asserted authority over the Collective, each offering a different account of the descent's purpose. The ice in Umbrsulcavus~'s secondary chamber records a physical breach line coinciding with the crisis — stone splitting along a fault that scarred the cavern as thoroughly as the dispute scarred Nightshelf governance.
    
    ## Course and Consequences
    
    The fracture was infrastructural as much as political. Miners refused to cross the disputed caverns to reach the forges. Forgemasters sealed their vents. For three seasons, the Flipper Accord — the trade compact binding Aurora Stack and Nightshelf — hung by obligation alone. Aurora watched from the surface, calculating whether the deep would collapse and take their fire-core supply with it.
    
    Iudicium∴culum, the emergency statute governing Nightshelf justice, proved inadequate: written to address external infiltration, it offered no mechanism for resolving internal succession. The crisis ultimately forced amendments extending the statute's authority to cover governance failures — one of the few lasting institutional reforms to emerge from the Fracta.
    
    Among the claimants, the ability known as Gnash and Crumble served as catalyst; the ice remembers the name associated with it because the fracture deepened in its wake. Resolution came through sacrifice — accounts agree it was necessary rather than noble, and the memorial tokens left at Umbra∴go, the Nightshelf commemorative rite, tell a story the living have chosen not to repeat aloud.
    
    ## Notable Death
    
    ☽'Otrolal the Watchful died during the fracturing, holding back darkness in the deep tunnels so others could maintain their fires. The body was recovered; the ancestor-bone clasp was returned to the family. What Otrolal contained in those tunnels — the nature of the threat — left marks on the Nightshelf that outlasted any memorial.
    
    The Collective☽ Of Led∴merch survived the Fracta. Its unity did not.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771595283743_0", anchor: "the Flipper Accord — the trade compact binding Aurora Stack and Nightshelf — hung by obligation alone", text: "Administrative ice from this period preserves three distinct draft renegotiations of the Accord, none of which appear in the public record. 'Hung by obligation alone' is the version both sides prefer. What actually occurred was emergency diplomacy conducted in secret, with terms that shifted substantially between drafts. The Accord did not passively survive. It was actively rewritten while its signatories publicly maintained the fiction of continuity.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771595283743_1", anchor: "Three claimants who had waited at the cavern threshold", text: "Three. Nightshelf succession disputes that resolve into exactly three factions appear in the record with a frequency that suggests narrative convention more than political reality. The ice in the secondary chamber records a physical breach line. It does not record a headcount.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771595283743_2", anchor: "written to address external infiltration, it offered no mechanism for resolving internal succession", text: "The inadequacy runs deeper than jurisdictional scope. My annotation on Iudicium∴culum's origin identifies that 'external infiltration' was itself a contested framing — Nightshelf survey teams were operating in orca-claimed waters before the incursions the statute was written to address. A statute built on a mischaracterized threat proves inadequate when turned inward. This is not irony. It is structural.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771595283743_3", anchor: "holding back darkness in the deep tunnels so others could maintain their fires", text: "During the Frozen Peace. Not the Orca Incursion, not a Long Dark boundary event. 'Darkness' in deep tunnels adjacent to Umbrsulcavus~ — where the ice preserves memories of abnormal depth — warrants specificity this entry does not provide. The marks it left on the Nightshelf are referenced but unidentified. I could not source them further. The Foundation Depths contain impressions from that cavern system I have declined to catalogue in full.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
